the rhine is shrinking shrinking so much  it now has a beach  germany's commercial artery carries  every cargo imaginable steel fuel  chemicals as far as the north sea but at  the busy port town of koblenz the barges  are now leaving three quarters empty  on the wall  there are different colors and that's  normal water level  now it's decreased very much they need  more ships for the same cargo that means  the ships are more expensive and  the  costs per tonne  are  increasing  the pinch point is reached downstream at  kaub some large barges can no longer  pass here  this isn't the bank of the river iron  that i'm standing on it's the bed of the  river rhine so much has the water  receded over the last couple of months  because of the lack of rainfall  and the ships that you can see passing  behind me would normally have about two  meters of water beneath them so that  that's about that much water but at the  minute  some parts have got as little as this  much water 40 centimeters below the  boats as they pass  and the land the rhine ribbons through  is equally parched for 30 years reinhoyt  honor has watched his vineyard slowly  desiccate he's noticed the insects and  the weeds changing he last saw rain on  june 6th  the steps that they are taking to try to  combat the hot weather on this vineyard  are quite extraordinary they're pumping  30 000 liters of water onto these young  vines every single day  they're also allowing the leaves to grow  bigger and to shelter  the grapes below they've got two planes  and two pilots on standby for the whole  of the summer in case there should be a  hailstorm which would damage the grapes  it's not just the wine harvested threat  his neighbors have lost a third of their  corn it's just stopped growing  we know that in any language  the rhine may for now still be teeming  with life  but many germans are questioning for how  much longer they will be able to rely  upon it as an economic lifeline
 

